    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p15_b2",
      "text": "We presented a systematic mechanistic interpretability analysis of LoRA adapter feature geometry\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.44,
        144.15,
        523.69,
        154.95
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        285,
        576,
        2094,
        619
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.8,
      "font_name": "CMR10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 96,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p15_b3",
      "text": "using Sparse Autoencoders. Our delta SAE framework \u2014 training SAEs on adapter-induced\nactivation deltas h\u2206= hadapted \u2212hbase \u2014 provides a mechanistically clean decomposition of\nadapter contributions.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        157.53,
        523.36,
        195.62
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        630,
        2093,
        782
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.02,
      "font_name": "CMR10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 198,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p15_b4",
      "text": "Three convergent analyses demonstrate that LoRA adapters operate in a feature subspace\ngeometrically separated from the base model: cosine similarity near random (0.071), principal\nangles consistently near 74\u00b0, and CKA(hb, h\u2206) \u22480.05\u20130.35 depending on layer. Adapter-specific\nSAEs achieve 46\u201386% lower reconstruction error than base model SAEs on held-out data,\nconfirming the geometric separation reflects genuine learned structure. Feature density scales\nwith rank while geometric novelty remains rank-invariant.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.61,
        203.63,
        524.84,
        282.37
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        814,
        2099,
        1129
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.02,
      "font_name": "CMR10",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 513,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p15_b5",
      "text": "These findings identify a monitoring gap in LoRA-based safety alignment: base-model inter-\npretability tools may be systematically blind to adapter-encoded representations. The delta\nSAE framework provides a practical tool for feature-level auditing of fine-tuned models.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.61,
        290.38,
        525.14,
        328.48
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        1161,
        2100,
        1313
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.02,
      "font_name": "CMR10",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 271,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p15_b6",
      "text": "Future work will pursue causal validation using strongly-differentiated models (gemma-2-9b-it),\nextend to misalignment detection in the style of Evans et al. [2025], and establish base-base SAE\nsimilarity baselines to rigorously bound the observed geometric separation.\n",
      "bbox": [
